>examine sketch
It is a drawing, in your own clumsy pencil, of a girl with a way of tilting her
head as if the world were a question she meant to answer later. Fenchurch. You
have drawn her at least forty times and never once got the tilt right. She
vanished in the middle of a hyperspace jump, which is a thing that happens, and
which nobody has ever satisfactorily explained to you.

[The score has just gone up by two points.]

>e

The Village Green
A trampled circle of grass ringed by a dozen houses like your own. Smoke rises,
unhurried, from one or two chimneys. Your house is to the west, the sandwich hut
stands to the east, and a path runs north to the meadow where the occasional
ship puts down.

>e

The Sandwich Hut
A low hut with a wide shutter that folds down into a counter. This is where your
life happens. Shelves and a cool pit hold the ingredients, a chopping board sits
in the middle of the counter, and the knife hangs from its nail. The village
green is back to the west.

On the counter is a sandwich knife. On the chopping board is a loaf of bread. In
the cool pit is a cured meat. On the pantry shelf are some sourberries and some
pickled roots.

Hob leans on the counter with the air of a man who has been waiting since before
the sun. 

>ask hob about order
"Same as always," says Hob. "Meat and sourberry, thick as you like. And no
pickle. I have views about pickle."

>take knife
Taken.

>take meat
Taken.

>put meat on board
You put the cured meat on the chopping board.

>take berries
Taken.

>put berries on board
You put the sourberries on the chopping board.

>make sandwich
You slice, stack and press, with the unhurried competence of long practice. The
result is a sandwich of meat and sourberries, between two thick slabs of
Lamuellan bread.

>give sandwich to hob
(first taking the sandwich)
Hob takes it in both hands, weighs it like a newborn and bites. For a while he
is quite unavailable for conversation. "That," he says at last, "is why we keep
you." He pays in a coin of no known denomination, shakes your hand, and strolls
off into the morning, chewing.

Far overhead, a thin whine builds, as of a mosquito with ambitions. Some minutes
later a child skids in to shout that the mail hopper has been, and has left a
parcel on the village green with the sandwich maker's name on it. Nobody on
Lamuella has ever received a parcel before. The hopper itself, you gather, has
landed on the meadow and is not yet sure it will leave.

[The score has just gone up by ten points.]

>w

The Village Green
A trampled circle of grass ringed by a dozen houses like your own. Smoke rises,
unhurried, from one or two chimneys. Your house is to the west, the sandwich hut
stands to the east, and a path runs north to the meadow where the occasional
ship puts down. Someone has left a brown parcel in the middle of the grass, as
if it were an offering.

You can see a brown parcel (which is closed) here.

>open parcel
You open the brown parcel, revealing a handwritten note and a Guide Mk II.

[The score has just gone up by five points.]

>take guide
Taken.

>read note
In Ford's scrawl: "Arthur. Mind this for me. Don't switch it on, don't believe a
word it says and don't let it near anyone who wants to be helped. Will collect.
Soon-ish. F."

A sound like tearing silk comes down out of the sky, and a sleek white shuttle
puts itself carefully down in the meadow. Presently two people come looking for
you: a woman, brisk and tired, and a girl who walks several paces behind with
her hands in her pockets as if she had been delivered against her will.

"Hello, Arthur," says the woman. You know her at once. Of all the people you did
not expect to see on Lamuella, she is the first. "This is Random. She's your
daughter."

She says it the way one reports a delay on a railway. The girl says nothing,
which she does with great force.

[The score has just gone up by two points.]

>switch on guide
The cube warms against your palm. A line of friendly text blooms on its face:
HELLO, ARTHUR. IT IS GOOD TO FINALLY BE SOMEWHERE WITH A VIEW. Beneath it, in
smaller type: YOU APPEAR TO BE HAPPY. THIS HAS BEEN NOTED.

[The score has just gone up by three points.]

>consult guide about hob order
THE GUIDE HAS ALREADY WORKED OUT WHAT HOB WANTS: MEAT AND SOURBERRIES, NO
PICKLES. YOU WERE ABOUT TO ASK. IT IS HAPPY TO SAVE YOU THE TROUBLE.

>consult guide about hob order
THE GUIDE HAS ALREADY WORKED OUT WHAT HOB WANTS: MEAT AND SOURBERRIES, NO
